opensbi: esp32s31: add a coprocessor dirty-state probe (SBI function 3)

Companion to patches/0029. Machine mode writes CLEAN to the PIE state CSR,
executes one vector load, and reads the state back, returning both readings
packed into the SBI return value so Linux can print them - the M-mode console
prints at a different baud from Linux's and lands in the unreadable region
where the console switches over.

Answer, on this silicon: wrote 2, read 2, and 3 after one vector instruction.
The hardware raises its own dirty state, so a lazy save is possible in
principle. See 0029 for why the obvious lazy save did not pay.

Apply against opensbi-esp32-s31.
